<h2 id="give-each-series-a-stable-colour">Give each device a stable colour</h2>

With the civica companion installed, each device on the Energy dashboard keeps its colour, pattern and marker. Adding, hiding or reordering devices does not change it.

Energy roles have fixed colours:

| Energy role | Colour | Pattern |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Grid import | Primary blue | Solid |
| Grid export | Blue shade 50% | Solid |
| Solar | Orange tint 25% | Solid |
| Gas | Primary magenta | Solid |
| Water | Teal tint 25% | Solid |
| Non-fossil energy | Teal tint 25% | Diagonal |
| Battery charging | Purple tint 25% | Solid |
| Battery discharge | Purple tint 25% | Reverse diagonal |
| Total consumption | Black (White in dark mode) | Solid, with a dashed line |

Colours repeat after 6 devices, so the pattern, line dash and marker tell them apart. [Charts and tables](../components/charts.html) explains crowded charts, and the [24-device chart](../components/chart-examples.html) shows one.

### For developers

Pass the device's stable entity ID or statistic ID to `chartSeriesStyle(key, {mode})` from `design-system/chart-colours.mjs`. It returns a categorical colour, fill pattern, line dash and marker. The same ID always gives the same style. Keep the ID when the friendly name changes. A previous-period comparison uses the same base ID. For several meters in one role, use keys like `gas:<statistic ID>`. Each keeps the role colour with its own pattern.

To change a chart colour or energy role, follow [Maintain the colour records](../community/index.html#maintain-colour-records).

<h2 id="bg-col-07">Choose colours from what the data means</h2>

<h3 id="choose-the-colour-mapping">Match the colours to what they show</h3>

| What the colour shows | Colours | What the chart also needs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A source, such as a device | The [categorical palette](#categorical-palette) | A stable ID, a readable name and a pattern, line style or marker |
| An amount that goes up in one direction | The tints and shades of one [scale family](#sequential-and-divergent-scale-palette) | The measure, unit, lowest and highest values, and what happens outside them |
| A difference above or below a centre | Two scale families with a neutral centre | A centre that means something, such as a target temperature, and signed values |
| No reading | None | Words such as “Not supplied” and a mark that is not a number |

Never use categorical colours for an amount. A real zero belongs on the scale. An unknown or unavailable reading does not.

### Define a scale before you choose colours

For a heatmap or map layer that shows an amount, first write down the quantity, unit, period, range and any centre. Say when a value falls outside the scale, because the end colour would hide it. If the range changes, update the key.

Keep labels outside coloured areas unless you have tested them on that fill. Add outlines to pale steps. Show a missing value as a crossed empty swatch marked “Not supplied”, never in a scale colour.

This example shows 2 heatmaps of one week: energy per room from 0 to 2 kWh, and room temperature against a 20°C target. The garage's 0.00 kWh takes the lightest step. A missing reading is a crossed empty cell.

<%= example('data-colour-quantitative') %>

## In Home Assistant

Each native card draws the civica chart colours its own way:

- **Energy dashboard cards**: with the optional civica companion, each role and device gets its style from this page, plus a **View data** table. Without it, devices take the numbered colours by position.
- **History graph and Statistics graph cards**: each series takes a numbered colour, `civica-chart-1` to `civica-chart-24`, by its position in the card. The theme also sets these as Home Assistant's `color-1` to `color-24`. Lines are solid, with no patterns or end labels, so give each entity a clear name. Reordering entities can change their colours. From the 25th series the card uses Home Assistant's default colours, wrapping after the 54th. In dark mode, the 1st, 4th, 10th, 11th, 16th, 17th, 22nd and 23rd series are hard to see, so check them in the legend.

The 24 numbered colours follow the energy roles first, then repeat the 6 categorical colours:

| Numbered colour | Colour | Energy role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `civica-chart-1` | Primary blue | Grid import |
| `civica-chart-2` | Primary magenta | Gas |
| `civica-chart-3` | Teal tint 25% | Non-fossil energy |
| `civica-chart-4` | Blue shade 50% | Grid export |
| `civica-chart-5` | Orange tint 25% | Solar |
| `civica-chart-6` | Teal tint 25% | Water |
| `civica-chart-7` | Purple tint 25% | Battery charging |
| `civica-chart-8` | Purple tint 25% | Battery discharge |
| `civica-chart-9` | Black (White in dark mode) | Total consumption |
| `civica-chart-10` to `civica-chart-24` | Primary blue, Blue shade 50%, Primary magenta, Purple tint 25%, Teal tint 25%, Orange tint 25%, repeated in that order | None |

This example shows a History graph card of humidity in 4 rooms, in light and dark mode. The first entity takes `civica-chart-1`, the second `civica-chart-2`, and so on.

<%= example('data-colour-categorical') %>

[Charts and tables](../components/charts.html) owns line widths and the zero line. [Data visualisation](brand-data.html) explains how to choose a chart.
